DB Conversion
-------------

Here are the steps required to convert a database dump from ecmspy.com
to sqlite3:

1. Import the ecmspy.com database backup into a new MySQL database:
  $ unzip <name-of-backup>.zip
  $ cat <name-of-sql>.sql | mysql -u<user> -p<password> <name-of-db>

2. Convert the MySQL Database into a SQLite script:
 $ sh scripts/mysql2sqlite.sh -u<user> -p<password> <name-of-db> > buelltune.sq3

3. Remove all column COMMENTs from the file generated above (not supported
   by SQLite3)

4. Create a new sqlite database
  $ sqlite3 assets/buelltune.db < buelltune.sq3

5. Compress the resulting db file with gzip, replacing the current version
  $ gzip -f assets/buelltune.db

6. Bump `version` in `@Database(...)` on `EcmDefinitionsDatabase`
  (`app/src/main/java/biz/logicminds/buelltune/data/EcmDefinitionsDatabase.kt`)
  whenever the entities change or the database content is regenerated.
7. Set `PRAGMA user_version = N` on the built database, matching the version
   from step 6, before gzipping it:
  $ sqlite3 assets/buelltune.db 'PRAGMA user_version = N;'

  Room opens this asset via `createFromAsset("buelltune.db")` (the filename
  has no `.gz` - aapt strips that extension when packaging the asset) instead
  of creating the database itself, then compares the copied file's `PRAGMA
  user_version` against `@Database(version = N)`. A mismatched or unset
  (default 0) `user_version` sends Room down an `onCreate`/migration path
  against tables that already exist; `EcmDefinitionsDatabase` declares no
  `Migration`s - this is a read-only bundled reference file, so a schema or
  content change ships an entirely new asset rather than migrating an
  existing install in place - so a mismatch fails hard with
  `IllegalStateException: A migration from <asset version> to <N> was
  required but not found` the first time any provider queries the database,
  i.e. on first app launch after the update installs.
